WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.200
Medical journals can cost
tens of thousands of dollars

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.640
per year for a subscription,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.120
and online articles,
single online articles,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.720
dozens of dollars each.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.240
But the founder of
the website Sci-Hub

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.430
sought to change that.
Check it out.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:22.092
"How to Access Research Articles
for Free with Sci-Hub"

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:30.080
The first issues of the first scientific
journals were published back in 1665,

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.040
in which it was noted things like, hey,
it looks like there's a spot on Jupiter,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.360
thanks to new telescopes
invented by a certain Mr. Newton,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.000
whose friend Halley described a comet.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.400
The same journal that reported that
oranges and lemons could cure scurvy,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.160
and something in willow tree
bark could bring down a fever.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.720
Also published, a letter by
some guy over in the colonies

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.880
about playing with kites
during lightning storms,

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.480
and an account of a remarkable 8-year-old
musician by the name of Amadeus,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.040
and within this last century some
sketchings of the structure

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.360
of some molecule called DNA.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.560
A journal still in publication
to this day, 350 years later,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.960
available now online and in print

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:26.480
for the low, low subscription
price of only $6,666 a year.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.880
As you can imagine, the
high price of journals

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.960
leaves doctors in developing countries

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.600
missing out on relevant
information about health.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.680
At that time, back in the '90s, there
was optimism that by 2004 at least,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.080
the problem of access to life-saving
scientific information would be solved.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.120
But 2004 came and went,
setting their sights for 2015.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.520
Surely by then we can achieve
health information for all,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.160
as lack of access
remained a major barrier.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.480
Realistically, only scientists at really
big, well-funded universities

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.560
in the developed world may have
full access to published research,

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.200
and as prices rise even higher,
even that may no longer be true.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.320
You know there's a problem
when even Harvard,

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.160
as in $30-billion-dollar-
endowment Harvard,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.840
claims that costs for research
journals are now prohibitive.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.400
Meanwhile, the journal
publishers are raking in billions

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.080
charging institutions up to
$35,000 a year per journal,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.440
and charging individuals
online per article.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.480
So you have a family member diagnosed
with some disease and you go online.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.200
You can read all sorts of internet drek,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.800
but if you want to see the actual
science, it can get expensive.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.880
And you likely paid for the research.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.280
Tax dollars pour in to fund the research,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.800
and then you can't get access
to the research you paid for.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.040
It's like if a nice little city park was built,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.640
but then some private firm came in
and started to charge admission.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.720
That's roughly how it works
with scientific research,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.920
and this conversion of public research
dollars into private publishing profits

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.560
has long been a source of discontent.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.160
The publishers don't end up
paying anything for the research.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.520
They get it for free; they don't
pay the researchers anything.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.680
So we pay for it, and then we have
to pay for it again if we want to read it.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.840
So it can end up with science as a profit
system, rather than science as knowledge.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:39.280
Enter Alexandra Elbakyan, nicknamed
by some the Robin Hood of Science.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.840
It's the story of how one researcher made
nearly every scientific paper ever published

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.920
available for free to everyone,
anywhere in the world.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.160
Named by perhaps the most prestigious
scientific journal in the world

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.720
as one of the top 10 people who
mattered the most in science in 2016,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.760
Alexandra started out as just a
frustrated grad student in Kazakhstan,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.760
unable to access the scholarly papers
she needed for her research.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.560
Once she figured out how to
circumvent all the paywalls,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.680
she started a website,
now at https://sci-hub.st/

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.800
to remove all barriers
in the way of science

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.840
by giving away the world's scientific,
medical, and nutrition literature for free.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.840
"What she did is nothing short
of awesome," said one researcher.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.280
"Lack of access to the scientific
literature is a massive injustice,

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.425
and she fixed it with one fell swoop."

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:49.440
Alexandra Elbakyan, a 20-
something year old grad student,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.400
is operating a free searchable
online database

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.160
of nearly 50 million stolen
scholarly journal articles,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.720
shattering the $10 billion-per-year
paywall of academic publishers,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.120
an awe-inspiring act of altruism,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.680
or a massive criminal enterprise,
depending on whom you ask.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.480
Now up to 60 million papers, providing
access to nearly all scholarly literature.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:20.960
Via its websites sci-hub.cc,
sci-hub.io, and sci-hub.ac,

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:27.337
Sci-Hub was able to fill 99.3%
of articles requests, all for free.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:34.480
A sister site, Library Genesis, at libgen.io,
distributes scientific books and textbooks

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.480
for free, more than a million
of them, also illegally.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.520
Who's downloading pirated papers?

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.240
Everyone, concluded this feature
in the prestigious journal, Science.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.880
A survey of potential users suggests for most

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.640
it's not some grand political statement,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.280
but rather that's the only
way they have access,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.440
or feel it's just so much quicker and easier.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.000
Even those who have legitimate institutional
access may still choose to use Sci-Hub

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.960
because there's just so many
fewer hoops to jump through.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.640
So you can imagine how
sites like Sci-Hub may

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.800
be filling publishers that charge for access

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.520
with roaring, existential panic,
and they're not taking it lying down.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.880
Elsevier, the largest publisher, notorious
for demanding researchers take down

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.800
free copies of their own work,

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.560
sued Sci-Hub, the Library
Genesis project, Alexandra,

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.200
and 99 John Does for copyright infringement,
a willful disregard of Elsevier's rights.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.840
Kind of hard to take the
moral high ground, though,

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.040
when you're effectively an
international arms dealer.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.320
Can you imagine a tobacco
company publishing health journals?

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.640
Surely the company's business mission
would be impossibly confused:

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.440
would the company be in the business
of killing people or keeping them alive?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.480
But if you can't imagine that absurdity,
well, welcome to Elsevier,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.400
which in addition to
publishing medical journals

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.720
is also involved in the global arms trade,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.640
running arms fairs where things
like cluster bombs are sold,

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.400
leading to medical journal editorial
boards calling for a boycott

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.400
of Elsevier's warmongering, health
damaging business practices.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.080
In response to the lawsuit,
Alexandra wrote a letter to the judge.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.160
She wanted to make it clear that not only
did Elsevier not create those papers,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.880
but that they don't pay researchers a penny.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.840
So it's not like pirated movie or song
where the content creator is losing out,

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.320
noting that no researcher had ever complained
that she was handing out their research.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.600
In fact, scientific authors
are typically thrilled

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.320
when their work gets more
out into the world.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.880
That's the whole point of science:
to be shared and built upon.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.760
In one fell swoop,
Alexandra created a portal

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.320
likely offering a greater level
of access to science

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:57.840
than any institution on Earth in history,
literally opening up a world of knowledge.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.640
And she's not backing down,
citing in her defense Article 27

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.920
of the United Nations Universal
Declaration of Human Rights,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.680
that everyone should have the right to
freely participate in the cultural life

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.320
of the community, including sharing in
scientific advancement and its benefits.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.760
She realizes she could be arrested and
extradited to the U.S. to face charges.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.400
She is fully aware that another
computer prodigy-turned-advocate,

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.760
Aaron Swartz, was arrested on similar charges
after mass downloading academic papers.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:39.840
Facing devastating financial penalties
and jail time, Swartz hanged himself.

00:08:40.255 --> 00:08:43.536
https://sci-hub.st/

